WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.337
Resizing heap memory is easy to get wrong
because a resize changes more than

2
00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:04.965
capacity.

3
00:00:05.045 --> 00:00:09.490
It can change the address, object layout
assumptions, aliases, and failure behavior.

4
00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:14.016
A realloc-style operation should be
treated as an ownership and lifetime transition.

5
00:00:14.096 --> 00:00:18.176
If resizing fails, the original allocation
remains the object the program must

6
00:00:18.256 --> 00:00:18.683
manage.

7
00:00:18.763 --> 00:00:22.782
Assigning the result directly to the only
owning pointer can lose the original

8
00:00:22.862 --> 00:00:24.080
allocation on failure.

9
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.423
A safer pattern preserves the original
pointer until success is confirmed and then

10
00:00:28.503 --> 00:00:29.843
updates ownership state.

11
00:00:29.923 --> 00:00:32.785
When resizing succeeds, the allocation may
have moved.

12
00:00:32.865 --> 00:00:35.971
Old aliases, interior pointers, cached
element addresses,

13
00:00:36.051 --> 00:00:38.487
and borrowed references may no longer be
valid.

14
00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:42.769
Code that resizes a buffer should review
every place that may still refer to the old

15
00:00:42.849 --> 00:00:43.336
storage.

16
00:00:43.416 --> 00:00:46.096
Pointer and size metadata must be updated
together.

17
00:00:46.176 --> 00:00:48.368
Capacity, current length, element count,

18
00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:51.250
and object state should describe the same
allocation.

19
00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:55.715
A mismatch between pointer and metadata
can make later accesses appear checked while

20
00:00:55.795 --> 00:00:57.317
still using the wrong bounds.

21
00:00:57.397 --> 00:01:01.112
A good resize workflow defines what
happens when expanding, shrinking,

22
00:01:01.192 --> 00:01:03.872
failing, moving, or receiving a zero-size
request.

23
00:01:03.952 --> 00:01:07.849
That definition makes resizing reviewable
instead of relying on scattered

24
00:01:07.929 --> 00:01:08.660
assumptions.
